To make an OpenPGP key:

  1. Plug the YubiKey and a FAT USB stick labelled KEYS.
  2. Set the clock, as the Pi has no battery-backed one:
       date -u -s 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'
  3. Run the key generator, /usr/bin/gpg-genkey:
       PASSPHRASE='...' gpg-genkey -n 'Your Name' -e you@example.org

The header comment of the script lists its options. The secret key is made
in memory, under /dev/shm, and leaves only on the stick, which systemd mounts
on /mnt/usb when something reads it and unmounts after 10 seconds of disuse:
wait that long before pulling it out.

To move the subkeys to the YubiKey, use `gpg --edit-key` and `keytocard`.
`gpg --card-status` and `ykman info` show what the YubiKey holds.
